$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Bromodifluoromethylation of aldehydes (General procedure). Lithium
bromide (22 mg, 0.25 mmol for 4a-g,i-k; 131 mg, 1.5 mmol for 4h,l) was
dried by heating with the heat gun (ca. 300 °C) under vacuum of oil pump
during 3 min, and the flask was cooled to room temperature and filled
with argon. Propionitrile (1 mL), Bu4NBr (177 mg, 0.55 mmol for 4ag, i-k;
483 mg, 1.5 mmol for 4h,l), aldehyde 2 (0.5 mmol), and silane 1a (305 mg,
1.5 mmol) were successively added, and the reaction mixture was heated
under gentle reflux (2 h for 4d,e; 5 h for 4a-c,f,g,i-k; 10 h for 4h,l).
The dark brown mixture was cooled to room temperature, trifluoroacetic
acid (154 μL, 2 mmol for 4a-g,i-k; 308 μL, 4 mmol for 4h,l) and KHF2 (78
mg, 1 mmol for 4a-g,ik; 156 mg, 2 mmol for 4h,l) were added, and the
mixture was stirred for 1 h at room temperature. For the work-up,
saturated aqueous Na2CO3 (1 mL) was added dropwise followed by addition
of water (5 mL), and the mixture was extracted with methyl tert-butyl
ether (3×3 mL). The combined organic phases were filtered through Na2SO4,
and concentrated under vacuum. The residue was dissolved in small amount
of hexane/EtOAc (2/1), passed through short (1 cm) silica gel pad washing
with 5 mL of hexane/EtOAc (2/1). The solvent was evaporated, and the
residue was purified by column chromatography on silica gel.
2-Bromo-2,2-difluoro-1-phenylethanol (4a). 4 Yield 114 mg (96%).
Colorless oil. Bp 98–102 °C (bath temp.)/0.55 Torr. Chromatography
hexanes/EtOAc 10 : 1 → 5 : 1. Rf 0.16 (hexanes/EtOAc 10 : 1). 1H NMR (300
MHz, CDCl3), δ: 2.87 (d, 1H, J = 3.8), 5.03 (ddd, 1H, J = 10.2, 7.3,
3.8), 7.38–7.47 (m, 3H), 7.48–7.55 (m, 2H). 13C NMR (75 MHz, CDCl3), δ:
78.7 (t, J = 24.9), 124.2 (dd, J = 311.8, 310.2), 128.0 (t, J = 1.2),
128.6, 129.6, 134.4 (d, J = 2.2). 19F NMR (282 MHz, CDCl3), δ: –60.0 (dd,
1F, J = 162.1, 10.2), –56.6 (dd, 1F, J = 162.1, 7.3). Calcd for C8H7BrF2O
(237.04): C, 40.54; H, 2.98. Found: C, 40.39; H, 3.06.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM alternative preparation shown
